# uv

# Initialize a new project:
uv init my-project

# Add a dependency (Installs the package and immediately adds it to pyproject.toml):
uv add requests

# Add a development-only dependency:
uv add --dev pytest

# Remove a dependency:
uv remove requests

# View your dependency tree:
uv tree

# Sync environment with your lockfile:
uv sync

# Update uv
uv self update

# uv manages virtual environments implicitly behind the scenes, so you do not need to manually activate an environment.
# Run a project script:
uv run main.py (Runs within the isolated project environment)

# Run a CLI tool package:
uv run flask run

# Running Global CLI Tools (Replaces pipx)If you need a global command-line tool (like black, ruff, or youtube-dl) but don't want it messing with your project dependencies, use the tool command or the uvx shortcut:

# Run a tool temporarily:
uvx ruff check

# Install a tool permanently to your global path:
uv tool install ruff

# List installed global tools:
uv tool list

# Uninstall a global tool:
uv tool uninstall ruff

# Legacy pip InterfaceIf you prefer standard pip workflows or are working on legacy infrastructure that relies on a requirements.txt file, uv offers a drop-in replacement interface that runs up to 100x faster than traditional pip:

# Create a traditional virtual environment: 
uv venv

# Install from a requirements file:
uv add -r requirements.txt

# Compile a requirements file (replaces pip-compile): 
uv pip compile pyproject.toml -o requirements.txt
